# tools/al/ales_stereo_dc_remove_witness.rish -- stereo_dc_remove: the DC-offset remover carried into stereo, two independent means taken back out, a per-channel pure translation that PRESERVES the stereo image (ALES155).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES155.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-060518_fill-ales155-lotus-stereo-dc-remove.md
# Stands on: lotus/dc_remove.rye (ALES90 -- the mono block-mean DC remover run once per channel, its dc_remove and DcError reused whole), lotus/stereo_rectify.rye (ALES138 -- the EVEN map whose per-channel DC offset this rung undoes, the composition proof, and
# whose image-COLLAPSE this rung's image-PRESERVE answers), lotus/rectify.rye (ALES84 -- the mono rectifier underneath ALES138), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, sample_min/max, and the one true saturate reused through ALES90).
#
# lotus/stereo_dc_remove.rye removes the DC offset from a span in both channels of a master, running ALES90's proven mono dc_remove on each -- each channel's OWN mean subtracted, the left correcting the left's offset and the right the right's. It validates the span once
# against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadRange), so a refusal never DC-removes one channel and leaves the other biased; DcError reused whole. The lift is length-preserving, so it meets no desync wall -- the two channels simply run their own removal.
# The crux is the stereo DC-remove law (each channel equals ALES90's mono dc_remove byte for byte), the independent-offset law (two different offsets each land under one LSB independently), the image-preserve law (an out-of-phase master riding a common offset returns still out
# of phase -- the honest opposite of the rectifier's collapse), and the composition law (it undoes the stereo rectifier's per-channel offset).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_dc_remove_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-dc-remove: Language EN — stereo_dc_remove removes the DC offset from a span in both channels of a stereo master, each channel's OWN mean subtracted, running the mono dc_remove on each: y equals saturate(x minus the mean of its own channel), a per-channel pure translation that lands each channel's span average on zero. The two channels carry their own offsets — an even map or an asymmetric clip can bias the left and the right by different amounts — so the left removes the left's offset and the right removes the right's, independently. It validates the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never DC-removes one channel and leaves the other biased. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; an empty span is a clean no-op on both."
say "ales-stereo-dc-remove: Style Radiant — ALES90 named dc_remove: subtract a span's mean from every sample, the block-mean remover every chain carries right after a nonlinearity, because a rectifier or asymmetric drive shifts the mean off zero and a DC offset eats headroom and thumps at a cut seam. Carried into stereo, the two channels carry their own offsets, so this rung removes each channel's own mean independently. It is the honest opposite of the stereo rectifier ALES138: the rectifier is an even map that collapses an out-of-phase master to mono, where DC removal is a per-channel translation that preserves every sample-to-sample difference, so an out-of-phase master riding a common-mode offset comes back still out of phase, the antisymmetry kept rather than collapsed. The lift is length-preserving, so it meets no desync wall unlike the silence edits ALES148 through ALES154 — each channel simply runs its own removal, and the balance invariant is never at risk because no length changes. The crux is five laws: the stereo DC-remove law (each channel equals ALES90's mono dc_remove over the same span byte for byte); the independent-offset law (two channels carrying different offsets each land under one LSB independently, the left's offset never leaking into the right, a zero-mean channel unchanged while its partner is corrected); the image-preserve law (an out-of-phase master riding a common offset returns still out of phase, a both-zero-mean master unchanged exactly, the map idempotent); the composition law (it undoes the stereo rectifier's per-channel offset — a symmetric master rectified through ALES138 rides a positive DC on both channels, and stereo_dc_remove restores a near-zero mean on each); and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (length held, a refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count zero and the empty span the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-dc-remove: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) DC-removed through the proven mono dc_remove, a two-pass block statistic per channel (sum in i64 so the accumulation never overflows, then subtract, one saturate per sample at the rail), memoryless, no carried state, no feedback, no delay line; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a remove writes values only, the length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the span is validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never DC-removes one channel and leaves the other biased; StereoDcError is ALES90's DcError (BadRange) reused whole, the lift adding no fault since the operation takes no coefficient. The stereo DC-remove law is proven against ALES90's mono dc_remove directly (a second call per channel) with a left biased plus 1100 and a right biased minus 500, each channel byte for byte; the independent-offset law proves a large left offset and a small right offset each removed to under one LSB independently and a zero-mean channel unchanged while its partner is corrected; the image-preserve law proves an out-of-phase master (right the negation of the left's AC) riding a common plus 1000 offset returns still exactly out of phase, a both-zero-mean master unchanged, and the map idempotent; the composition law proves a symmetric master rectified through ALES138 rides a positive DC of 18000 on the left and 12000 on the right, each removed to under one LSB; the span discipline proves a sub-span removal leaves the outer samples untouched on both channels; and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove the length held, a bad span refusing BadRange with both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the DC being the arithmetic mean of the block (the zero-frequency component by definition, not a claim about any hardware highpass), no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_dc_remove"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_dc_remove.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-dc-remove: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_dc_remove.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-dc-remove: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-dc-remove: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-dc-remove' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-dc-remove: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-dc-remove line"
